WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.894
A California city moves residential parking permits online and announces that the new service is faster.

2
00:00:06.894 --> 00:00:07.739
Faster for whom?

3
00:00:07.739 --> 00:00:13.498
A resident with broadband, a credit card, stable housing, and flexible work hours may finish in five minutes.

4
00:00:13.498 --> 00:00:20.894
A resident sharing a phone, paid by the hour, or living at an address that does not match a vehicle record may face a very different system.

5
00:00:20.894 --> 00:00:23.905
That gap is both a civic problem and a sociology problem.

6
00:00:23.905 --> 00:00:25.547
This is preparation only.

7
00:00:25.547 --> 00:00:29.487
Listening does not create college credit or transfer credit.

8
00:00:29.487 --> 00:00:34.762
Sociology asks how individual lives connect to institutions and patterned relationships.

9
00:00:34.762 --> 00:00:43.573
Start with social stratification, the ranking of people and groups through unequal access to money, power, status, education, health, and security.

10
00:00:43.573 --> 00:00:46.030
Income is money received during a period.

11
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.548
Wealth is the value of assets after debts.

12
00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:51.005
They are related but not interchangeable.

13
00:00:51.005 --> 00:00:58.318
Two households with similar paychecks may have very different abilities to survive a medical bill or a month without work.

14
00:00:58.318 --> 00:01:01.555
Stratification can take forms such as caste and class.

15
00:01:01.555 --> 00:01:06.050
A class system allows some movement, but movement is not equally available.

16
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:09.347
Social mobility can be upward, downward, or horizontal.

17
00:01:09.347 --> 00:01:14.981
A success story may show that movement is possible without proving that the structure is fair.

18
00:01:14.981 --> 00:01:20.316
Sociologists therefore compare life chances across groups, not just inspiring exceptions.

19
00:01:20.316 --> 00:01:23.553
Global inequality extends the question across nations.

20
00:01:23.553 --> 00:01:29.487
Countries differ in income, infrastructure, health, education, and their position in world markets.

21
00:01:29.487 --> 00:01:32.064
National averages hide internal inequality.

22
00:01:32.064 --> 00:01:35.361
Modernization theory emphasizes industrial development.

23
00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:40.516
Dependency approaches emphasize colonial histories and unequal economic relationships.

24
00:01:40.516 --> 00:01:43.093
Each directs attention to different causes.

25
00:01:43.093 --> 00:01:46.450
Race and ethnicity also shape institutions and outcomes.

26
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:52.024
Race is a socially constructed system that assigns meaning to perceived physical differences.

27
00:01:52.024 --> 00:01:56.759
Ethnicity refers to shared cultural practices, ancestry, language, or identity.

28
00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:02.034
The fact that race is socially constructed does not mean its consequences are imaginary.

29
00:02:02.034 --> 00:02:09.227
Laws, housing markets, schools, and enforcement practices can produce durable effects from categories societies created.

30
00:02:09.227 --> 00:02:10.965
Prejudice concerns attitudes.

31
00:02:10.965 --> 00:02:12.823
Discrimination concerns action.

32
00:02:12.823 --> 00:02:21.394
Institutional discrimination appears when normal rules or routines create unequal results, even without an openly biased person at the counter.

33
00:02:21.394 --> 00:02:25.350
The terms majority and minority describe power as well as numbers.

34
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:31.524
A numerical majority can have less authority, and a numerical minority can control important resources.

35
00:02:31.524 --> 00:02:34.761
Stereotypes simplify whole groups into assumed traits.

36
00:02:34.761 --> 00:02:37.878
Scapegoating assigns blame to a less powerful group.

37
00:02:37.878 --> 00:02:44.172
Assimilation, pluralism, segregation, and expulsion describe very different relationships between groups.

38
00:02:44.172 --> 00:02:48.367
A civic analyst should not treat them as points on one harmless scale.

39
00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:50.465
Gender is another social structure.

40
00:02:50.465 --> 00:02:58.018
Sex usually refers to biological characteristics, while gender refers to social meanings, identities, roles, and expectations.

41
00:02:58.018 --> 00:03:03.053
Gender socialization occurs through families, schools, peers, media, and workplaces.

42
00:03:03.053 --> 00:03:07.968
It can influence unpaid care, jobs, and whose behavior is judged as authoritative.

43
00:03:07.968 --> 00:03:11.084
Sexual orientation is distinct from gender identity.

44
00:03:11.084 --> 00:03:16.899
Intersectionality explains how class, race, gender, age, disability, and other positions combine.

45
00:03:16.899 --> 00:03:20.915
Families are social institutions as well as personal relationships.

46
00:03:20.915 --> 00:03:26.129
Societies define kinship, marriage, parenting, and household membership in varied ways.

47
00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:32.003
A nuclear family includes parents and children, while an extended family includes other relatives.

48
00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:40.155
Families provide care, socialization, emotional support, and economic cooperation, but they can also contain conflict and unequal labor.

49
00:03:40.155 --> 00:03:50.165
Marriage rates, divorce, cohabitation, same-sex families, single-parent households, and chosen families all show that one household model cannot stand in for everyone.

50
00:03:50.165 --> 00:03:52.563
Population study adds demographic tools.

51
00:03:52.563 --> 00:03:57.598
Fertility, mortality, and migration change the size and composition of a population.

52
00:03:57.598 --> 00:04:02.573
The crude birth rate and crude death rate count events relative to population size.

53
00:04:02.573 --> 00:04:07.188
Life expectancy summarizes expected years under current mortality conditions.

54
00:04:07.188 --> 00:04:15.280
Demographic transition theory describes a broad movement from high birth and death rates toward lower rates as societies industrialize.

55
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.457
It is a model, not a timetable every country follows.

56
00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:23.432
Urbanization is the growth of cities and the share of people living in urban areas.

57
00:04:23.432 --> 00:04:32.123
Cities concentrate opportunity, government, culture, and services, but also congestion, high land costs, displacement, and environmental burdens.

58
00:04:32.123 --> 00:04:37.577
Suburbanization and later movement back toward city centers can rearrange jobs and housing.

59
00:04:37.577 --> 00:04:42.912
Gentrification may bring investment while also raising rents and changing who can remain.

60
00:04:42.912 --> 00:04:48.307
An analyst should ask who receives the benefits, who bears the costs, and who had a voice.

61
00:04:48.307 --> 00:04:52.682
Environmental sociology connects human organization to the natural world.

62
00:04:52.682 --> 00:04:57.477
Pollution, heat, flooding, and hazardous land uses are not distributed randomly.

63
00:04:57.477 --> 00:05:03.651
Environmental racism describes disproportionate environmental burdens placed on racialized communities.

64
00:05:03.651 --> 00:05:12.402
The tragedy of the commons explains how individually rational use of a shared resource can damage everyone when rules and accountability are weak.

65
00:05:12.402 --> 00:05:17.317
Sustainable policy tries to meet present needs without destroying future capacity.

66
00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:19.595
Social change can be gradual or rapid.

67
00:05:19.595 --> 00:05:26.368
Technology, population shifts, environmental pressure, conflict, and social movements can all alter institutions.

68
00:05:26.368 --> 00:05:32.842
Collective behavior includes less structured group activity, while social movements pursue sustained change.

69
00:05:32.842 --> 00:05:36.618
Movements need grievances, but grievances alone are not enough.

70
00:05:36.618 --> 00:05:43.151
Resources, leadership, networks, political opportunities, and persuasive framing affect whether action grows.

71
00:05:43.151 --> 00:05:45.369
Reform movements seek limited change.

72
00:05:45.369 --> 00:05:48.486
Revolutionary movements seek a broad transformation.

73
00:05:48.486 --> 00:05:51.543
Resistance movements try to stop or reverse change.

74
00:05:51.543 --> 00:05:54.041
Now return to the California parking portal.

75
00:05:54.041 --> 00:05:58.469
The city should examine the service as an institution, not merely as software.

76
00:05:58.469 --> 00:06:06.247
Eligibility rules, identity checks, payment methods, counter hours, language access, and enforcement all distribute benefits and burdens.

77
00:06:06.247 --> 00:06:13.740
A portal may be technically available while functionally unavailable to someone without a compatible device or stable documentation.

78
00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:19.247
Build an equity review using evidence that local government can lawfully and responsibly collect.

79
00:06:19.247 --> 00:06:25.889
Measure starts, completions, abandonment points, processing time, appeal rates, and use of phone or counter channels.

80
00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:33.667
Break results down where privacy and sample size permit, such as language, neighborhood, device type, age band, or accommodation request.

81
00:06:33.667 --> 00:06:37.527
Do not infer race from a name or immigration status from a language.

82
00:06:37.527 --> 00:06:40.763
Do not publish tiny groups that could identify residents.

83
00:06:40.763 --> 00:06:47.916
Family structure matters because a permit tied only to the leaseholder may exclude another adult who drives the household car.

84
00:06:47.916 --> 00:06:52.004
Gender matters when unpaid caregiving makes daytime appointments harder.

85
00:06:52.004 --> 00:06:55.807
Class matters when a fee or unpaid hour creates a larger sacrifice.

86
00:06:55.807 --> 00:07:02.279
Race and ethnicity may matter through residential patterns, language access, or prior experience with enforcement.

87
00:07:02.279 --> 00:07:06.821
Age and disability may affect device use or the need for an alternative process.

88
00:07:06.821 --> 00:07:10.057
Intersectionality warns that these barriers can compound.

89
00:07:10.057 --> 00:07:12.271
Population and urban change matter too.

90
00:07:12.271 --> 00:07:17.437
A city with more multigenerational households may have more drivers than off-street spaces.

91
00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:22.376
New apartments may add residents under different parking rules than nearby older homes.

92
00:07:22.376 --> 00:07:24.987
Extreme heat may make an outdoor queue unsafe.

93
00:07:24.987 --> 00:07:28.394
A new transit line may change car ownership and curb demand.

94
00:07:28.394 --> 00:07:30.097
These are empirical questions.

95
00:07:30.097 --> 00:07:37.477
Use census data, administrative records, surveys, public comments, and direct usability testing, while stating the limits of each.

96
00:07:37.477 --> 00:07:40.088
Anecdotes and trends serve different purposes.

97
00:07:40.088 --> 00:07:42.813
One resident's ticket can reveal a failure mode.

98
00:07:42.813 --> 00:07:44.573
It cannot establish prevalence.

99
00:07:44.573 --> 00:07:47.185
A citywide completion rate can estimate scale.

100
00:07:47.185 --> 00:07:50.932
It can hide a neighborhood or language group with severe drop-off.

101
00:07:50.932 --> 00:07:54.054
Use both without allowing either to do the other's job.

102
00:07:54.054 --> 00:07:57.233
The civic-tech response should be specific and testable.

103
00:07:57.233 --> 00:08:00.355
Keep a staffed counter and phone option during rollout.

104
00:08:00.355 --> 00:08:03.194
Accept more than one form of address verification.

105
00:08:03.194 --> 00:08:06.260
Provide translated instructions and accessible design.

106
00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.190
Warn residents before enforcement.

107
00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:12.391
Publish a privacy statement explaining what data are collected and shared.

108
00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:15.513
Review results after sixty and one hundred eighty days.

109
00:08:15.513 --> 00:08:18.749
If disparities appear, change the process and test again.

110
00:08:18.749 --> 00:08:21.644
Social change is visible inside this small project.

111
00:08:21.644 --> 00:08:27.889
Moving a service online changes routines, staff roles, resident expectations, and the evidence a city can see.

112
00:08:27.889 --> 00:08:30.671
It may reduce travel and waiting for many people.

113
00:08:30.671 --> 00:08:36.405
It may also make residents with low digital access less visible because they never complete the form.

114
00:08:36.405 --> 00:08:39.811
Good administration does not assume innovation is equitable.

115
00:08:39.811 --> 00:08:46.510
It defines the intended benefit, watches distribution, and preserves a workable path for people the new system misses.

116
00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.831
Three recall questions.

117
00:08:47.831 --> 00:08:52.711
First, how are prejudice, discrimination, and institutional discrimination different?

118
00:08:52.711 --> 00:08:56.731
Second, what three demographic processes directly change a population?

119
00:08:56.731 --> 00:09:01.669
Third, what evidence should a city examine before calling a digital service equitable?

120
00:09:01.669 --> 00:09:10.984
First, prejudice is an attitude, discrimination is an action, and institutional discrimination occurs when established rules or routines produce unequal outcomes.

121
00:09:10.984 --> 00:09:15.181
Second, fertility, mortality, and migration directly change a population.

122
00:09:15.181 --> 00:09:27.428
Third, the city should examine completion and drop-off, time and errors, appeals, alternative-channel use, and differences among affected groups where measurement is lawful, private, and statistically responsible.

123
00:09:27.428 --> 00:09:32.028
It should combine those patterns with resident experience and usability testing.

124
00:09:32.028 --> 00:09:37.537
Artifact: Write a one-page equity and urban-change briefing for the parking portal.

125
00:09:37.537 --> 00:09:52.870
Include the institutional rule, the groups likely to encounter different barriers, one population or neighborhood trend, one environmental consideration, the evidence you would collect, and one response measurable after sixty days.

126
00:09:52.870 --> 00:10:14.907
